# Evidence-fragment retention policy (final-review I2/M8, resolved as part of
# the fragment-retention cleanup): traceability/runs/*.json is generated,
# per-run output -- every real module run (via bin/run-*.sh or ModuleRunner)
# writes a fresh <ISO-instant>-<archetype>.json fragment there, and letting
# every run-ever fragment accumulate untracked (194+ and growing across this
# wave's development) is what made I2's stale-fragment false-pass reachable
# in the first place: a fragment lookup that resolves "newest file in the
# directory" has a much larger blast radius when the directory never stops
# growing. Gitignoring the directory removes that exposure at its root
# rather than only patching the symptom (I2's fix bounds the lookup by
# run-start time; this removes the accumulation that made the bound
# necessary to begin with).
#
# git does NOT retroactively untrack a file just because it is later added to
# .gitignore, so this does not touch the 5 fragments already committed here
# (all TST-043, added as named fixtures/examples before this policy) -- they
# stay committed and tracked exactly as they are. Going forward, no new
# fragment should be added to git; they are ephemeral evidence output, not
# committed artifacts.
#
# Paths below are relative to this .gitignore's own directory (qe-harness/),
# per git's usual rule -- NOT re-prefixed with "qe-harness/".
traceability/runs/*.json

# test_acceptance_criteria.yml is likewise fully regenerated (by
# bin/merge-fragments.py, from the runs/ fragments above) -- generated
# output, not a committed artifact. Not preserved by CI's `artifacts:`
# block either (final-review M8).
traceability/test_acceptance_criteria.yml
